UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 34, Lesson 2: Rehearsing a dance', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Rehearsing means practising a dance over and over to improve it. Start slowly and make sure the order is right. Then work on one thing at a time, such as making the shapes clearer, keeping the timing or showing the character. Ask a friend or family member to watch and give feedback as a star and a wish. Make one change after each rehearsal and write it down. Drink water, stretch and rest between runs. A well-rehearsed dance looks confident and tells the story clearly.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nAfter a rehearsal, your viewer says they could not tell when the dance ended. Write one change and say how you will check that it worked.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Hold the final shape for three counts and look at the audience. I will rehearse again and ask them if the end is clear.'' A held final shape shows the ending, and checking with a viewer shows if it worked.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that more rehearsals without any change will fix problems. Rehearsal works best when each run has one clear focus and one improvement.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRehearse your story dance three times with a different focus each time. After each run write one change you made and one new problem. Ask a family member to give a star and a wish after the third run.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":nu