s (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise rehearsal.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use rehearsal with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a focus and explain why it matters.","I can practise feedback independently on a new prompt.","I can notice an improvement needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Rehearsing means practising a dance over and over to improve it. Start slowly and make sure the order is right. Then work on one thing at a time, such as making the shapes clearer, keeping the timing or showing the character. Ask a friend or family member to watch and give…","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Rehearsing means practising a dance over and over to improve it. Start slowly and make sure the order is right. Then work on one thing at a time, such as making the shapes clearer, keeping the timing or showing the character. Ask a friend or family member to watch and give feedback as a star and a wish. Make one change after each rehearsal and write it down. Drink water, stretch and rest between runs. A well-rehearsed dance looks confident and tells the story clearly.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about rehearsal?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Jade rehearses ''The Seed'' three times. Run 1: she corrects the order of the movements. Run 2: her focus is on holding the curled shape for three counts at the start. Run 3: her focus is on the big flower shape at the end. A friend says, ''Star: I could see the seed growing. Wish: hold the last shape longer.'' She writes the change in her notes.\n\nTRY FIRST\nAfter a rehearsal, your viewer says they could not tell when the dance ended. Write one change and say how you will check that it worked.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Hold the final shape for three counts and look at the audience. I will rehearse again and ask them if the end is clear.'' A held final shape shows the ending, and checking with a viewer shows if it worked.","explain":"Rehearsing means practising a dance over and over to improve it. Start slowly and make sure the order is right. Then work on one thing at a time, such as making the shapes clearer, keeping the timing or showing the character. Ask a friend or family member to watch and give feedback as a star and a wish. Make one change after each rehearsal and write it down. Drink water, stretch and rest between runs. A well-rehearsed dance looks confident and tells the story clearly.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that more rehearsals without any change will fix problems. Rehearsal works best when each run has one clear focus and one improvement. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Rehearsal focus.","caption":"One focus each run.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Run 1</text><text x=\"28\